-- work/goals.cub — the goals of the work on this repository (2026-11-12). Loaded after
-- lib/work.cub. Each goal's statement is the comment above its line in `serves`; a goal is reached
-- when its statement holds, and the goals it serves are the reason it is pursued. The tickets that
-- serve each goal are in work/tickets.cub (`python3 tools/work.py goals`).
data Goal = publish | self-aware | tooling | core-correct | typed-codes | partial-evaluation | native | typed-checker | work-object
serves : Goal → List Goal
-- publish (2026-11-12): the paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf) is published with its companion
-- repository, whose language core is complete: the formal language implemented exactly, the
-- language's own procedures carrying their correctness in their types, partial evaluation with the
-- Futamura projections, and realization as native code through C. Everything currently pursued
-- serves this.
serves publish = nil
-- self-aware (2026-11-12): an agent arriving with no context lands in a repository that states its
-- own reality: what it is, what is checked, what is assumed and where, what is wrong or not yet
-- known (open tickets, at the places concerned), and what is being done now (README.md, top).
-- Reached when every file has been read against the paper and the published calculus, every finding
-- is a ticket at its site, and the entry document says only what is true.
serves self-aware = cons publish nil
-- tooling (2026-11-12): the work's record is data of the language: goals and tickets checked
-- definitions (lib/work.cub), each ticket's text at its site, tools/work.py to list, show and check
-- them; queries written in the language itself follow. It serves the self-aware repository and is
-- itself a demonstration of the language as an information environment.
serves tooling = cons self-aware (cons publish nil)
-- core-correct (2026-11-12): the implementation is exactly the published calculus, CCHM (Cohen,
-- Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg 2018) with clocked guarded computation (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi
-- 2022): every rule present, none added, and the part that must be trusted small and plainly those
-- rules, with everything else (elaboration, sharing, memoization, specialization, translation)
-- either checked by that part or unable by construction to change a result.
serves core-correct = cons publish nil
-- typed-codes (2026-11-12): the language's own terms as typed objects in it, Code(Γ, A) = Σ c.
-- Der(Γ, c, A) (paper §6.1), over the one existing representation of terms; every definition
-- reflects to one; normalization as a term with its soundness equation (6.5).
serves typed-codes = cons partial-evaluation (cons native (cons typed-checker nil))
-- partial-evaluation (2026-11-12): the specializer with its law in its type (6.6), the first, second
-- and third Futamura projections (6.6–6.7), and specialization when values arrive at run time, the
-- residual checked, realized and cached with its evidence (paper §9.3).
serves partial-evaluation = cons publish nil
-- native (2026-11-12): realization through C, emitting C that keeps the program's structure so the
-- C compilers' optimizations apply: the translation returns the relation between running the C and
-- the term's meaning; the evaluator substitutes native code only with that relation; a process
-- becomes an executable whose system-call events are the process's interaction. The C compiler, the
-- C library and the hardware are trusted.
serves native = cons publish nil
-- typed-checker (2026-11-12): the checker returns a typing derivation or a refusal, so it is sound by
-- construction; then it is shown to decide derivability.
serves typed-checker = cons core-correct nil
-- work-object (2026-11-12, worked out with the user in one session; this is its record, and it is in
-- use from this commit on). The single knowledge interface to the work, used continuously by agents
-- and people. The symptom it removes is active now: an agent's or a conversation's knowledge lives
-- in an accumulating transcript that is lost or lossily summarised at every context reset and every
-- hand-off, and the codebase, the project's record and a session's working memory are different
-- things in different places.
--
-- The object. The work is one sequence of declarations in the language; the codebase is one region
-- of it. Everything said by anyone, person or agent, is a declaration: a definition, raw text, a
-- question, a decision, a correction, an answer. A conversation with the work is more declarations
-- in the same sequence, so it is an ongoing conversation within the code. There is no second format
-- and no separate store of notes. Files and directories are not its organisation: they are only how
-- declarations are stored today.
--
-- Raw text is a term, not an outer layer: a statement parsed as far as it has been parsed. Parsing
-- it further gives another statement ("this text says this"), as checkable as its form allows; the
-- boundary between formal and informal moves as parsing improves and nothing else changes. A
-- question parsed fully is a type, and it is settled by inhabiting or refuting it.
--
-- No imposed ontology. The base has no kinds of statement, no fixed relations, no notion of author,
-- session, ticket, goal or settled. Ticket, goal, question are types the work defines for itself
-- (lib/work.cub, this file). A relation is a reference one declaration makes to another by key, as a
-- definition references a definition; reverse relations are computed. Who said something, if it
-- matters, is itself a statement.
--
-- Open and closed loops. The current state of the work holds exactly what is live and worth
-- keeping. Anything opened (a question, a side investigation, a plan item, a note) is closed when it
-- is deemed completely unnecessary, at which point keeping it would only pollute the information
-- space: it is removed from the state, and anything that matters from it (an answer, a fact, a
-- correction) is folded into where it belongs. Anyone closes a loop; there is no owner. A loop may
-- be settled by computation (its question is now inhabited, what it waited on now checks); settling
-- obliges closing it, never leaving it hidden in place. Personal and collaborative loops are the
-- same thing.
--
-- History. The sequence of states over time is itself a mathematical object, kept as history (as a
-- repository's commits keep a deleted file): closing is reversible, but history is not content, and
-- no view reads it unless a past state is asked for explicitly. Store entries no current state
-- references are collected (#gc).
--
-- Identity and names. A declaration is identified by the content key of its canonical form (binders
-- nameless, every reference replaced by the referenced definition's key), as the store already does
-- for definitions. A binding is the statement that a name denotes a key; a name means its latest
-- binding; what was checked against an earlier binding keeps that binding's key, its meaning having
-- been fixed when it was checked. "Depends on a superseded binding" is computed; updating dependents
-- is elaborating them again under the new binding, which is more declarations. In the machine
-- today, replacing a binding is #retract followed by a new declaration (fixed by t56's close).
--
-- Merging. Declarations never change once written, so two histories merge by union, which always
-- commutes: content never conflicts. The only disagreement is a name with two latest bindings: a
-- computed fact, settled by a later declaration. This is why Pijul is not used: its theory of
-- patches (order-independent merges, conflicts as recorded states) exists because files are lines
-- edited in place; content identity gives the same properties here directly, and adopting it would
-- mean leaving GitHub and versioning lines of a representation the work is leaving.
--
-- Derived facts, ambient. Every fact about the work is an evaluation by the evaluator: a type, a
-- check, a refusal and its reason, a normal form, a cost, what depends on what and what depends on
-- it, the trust footprint, whether a standing constraint holds, whether a question is settled, what
-- a view shows. Inputs are identified by content, so a changed input has a different key and a
-- remembered result never goes stale: nothing is invalidated and no dependency tracking is needed
-- (the standard incremental-computation designs, Salsa, Adapton, build traces, track reads because
-- their inputs are mutable; here they are not). The evaluator already keys every term by its
-- structure and keeps one value per live key. What is needed: everything durable in canonical form
-- (a reference is the referenced definition's content key; run positions and names are only the
-- current state's rendering); the evaluator's key for a reference the content key (t60); results
-- kept beyond the run, stored canonically and rendered through the current bindings when read, the
-- same mechanism as the store's definitions (t61). Then the store's certificates are such kept
-- results (the checker applied to a definition's key, giving ok); the elaborator's solving rounds
-- are remembered; refusals are kept as acceptances are (t43); the checker's identity is the content
-- keys of its own definitions (t42); the per-file cache and its second root of trust go (t41, t44).
--
-- Views. A view is a term of the language applied to the work (t62: the work as a value); its value
-- is whatever its type says, presentation being separate. Codebase, project and session are the same
-- object and mechanism at different time scales; they differ only in which declarations a view
-- includes and how often it refreshes: the definitions reachable from what is wanted; the open goals
-- and tickets with what they serve; what a session's aim still needs. A session is a view given an
-- aim, which is a statement. The live working document of a session is its view: small, holding
-- exactly the open loops. Its budget (a screen for a person, a context window for an agent) forces
-- compression, and compression is refactoring: one declaration replacing several and saying so, as
-- several definitions are generalised into one.
--
-- Use. Agents and people use the work continuously through the machine's serve mode (blocks of
-- declarations and readings on standard input; a failing request does not stop it): declare, ask
-- readings, read views. An agent reads its views at the start of each exchange, so what it works
-- from is the work, not a transcript, and losing a conversation loses nothing. Many agents write
-- through one machine, which serialises them. Lasting corrections are standing declarations, and
-- whether a declaration respects them is a computed fact the moment it lands (what manual audits of
-- agents imitate). People see the same views through every faculty an editor offers: hover a
-- computed fact, go-to a reference, diagnostics refusals, outline a view, history the states, search
-- a query. The conversation is one more rendering.
--
-- Trust. The trusted part stays the checker and the evaluator. Everything producing declarations is
-- untrusted. Formal statements carry certificates; informal ones carry the statement of who said
-- them, so the line between verified and said is visible on every statement.
--
-- Exists: content identity (src/store.c), the names log with history, certificates, readings
-- (#type, #where, #scope, #key, #names, #history, #safe, #show), serve mode (src/lang.c, serve),
-- #retract, goals and tickets as types, the evaluator's structural keys. Missing, about 500 lines:
-- t60, t61, t62, t43, a way to write text as a term (t63), and the work record itself rebuilt on
-- this (t64). Not part of it (each was proposed in the founding session and withdrawn as imposed):
-- a program that calls a model and builds its context; an author field; fixed kinds of change; a
-- fixed set of relations; views that must produce text; ordering by causal references; a driver
-- script and session file; editor features and the moving of prose files as scheduled items; "keep
-- everything said".
serves work-object = cons tooling (cons self-aware (cons publish nil))
